I'm still very excited about this ascension, but I'll try not to
babble.

I'm pretty good at the protection racket, and I think it's obligatory
for monks. The entrance to the Mines was on DL 2, which made it
easier. The Minetown temple was coaligned, and I made good use
of it. Izchak's shop had a cursed magic lamp, which was quite
possibly the reason I ascended. I always do Minetown, then
Sokoban, then Mines End. By the time I finish this sequence
and start exploring lower in the main dungeon, my monks are
usually running into the difficulties of the vegan conduct. Hunger
starts to become a problem. This time, after finishing the Mines
I just traded in the lamp for a =oSD, which stayed on my finger
up until it was time to wake Rodney. Sokoban gave me a BoH,
which I prefer; reflection's never been a priority of mine. Below
Minetown I finally found a magic whistle, a great relief as I had
3 pets by then. I was surprised at how long it took to acquire
a unicorn horn. Usually I get one in the Mines, but this time I
found my first unicorn somewhere around DL 12.

I found the quest portal on DL 11, the earliest I've ever seen it.
I popped in and took out a few Xs and Es, but I returned quickly.

On DL 15 I converted an altar, and this became my base. I
spent a long time camped at the altar, my 10 or so pets
waiting patiently a level above or beneath. In my other games
altars have been for saccing for artifacts, but with a weaponless
monk I was praying for other goodies. I was really annoyed
when a well-meaning sacrifice got me Cleaver! I put together
a good collection of spellbooks, got telepathy, a few golden
glows, the castle tune, more protection. Getting crowned
didn't stop me; just slowed me down slightly. Finally I
ventured forth again. By this time the level had been so
chewed up by umber hulks and rock moles, and so much
stuff was lying around, you would have thought I had been
pudding farming. I left my main stash there.

DL 20 had an interesting bones pile. There was a greased
BoH there, as well as a chest. There were a couple of
wands of death, a half dozen magic markers (recharged
and mostly spent), blessed +5 speed boots (!!!), an amulet
of reflection, and the blessed +6 Staff of Aesculapius (not
much of a temptation to this weaponless monk). There was
a wand of polymorph (0:1), the only one I found this game.
There was also a throne room on this level, and the throne
let me genocide mind flayers.

DL 22 had the portal to Ludios. I took 6 pets in, and lost
all but 2. I disarmed every landmine and left them in a pile.
Then I took all the gold to Minetown and got some more
protection.

DL 23 was the Medusa level. I don't remember quite how
it happened, but right after I went down I attacked a troll,
and got a message about hitting it with my pickaxe! It
was very frustrating breaking weaponless combat, but I
just pretended it hadn't happened and went back to my
martial arts.

DL 27 was the castle. I took to smashing creatures
merrily with the passtune, but some wiseacre put a stop
to that with a wand of striking. So I went in the back door
with =levitation. Cleared out the castle, then the Valley.
Next thing I tried was just exploring the quest. I fell
through a trap door to Master Kaen's lair, but drank
cursed !oGL before anything could happen. I knew I
wasn't ready to face him. In one of the filler levels I
finally found my first polymorph trap, so I started
bringing my cats, dogs, and horses to try to get
some better pets. When one cat turned into a baby
green dragon I made a mistake with the Call command
(it didn't make sense to call him Toonces anymore) and
stepped onto the trap myself. I turned into a troll and lost
my starting robe. I threw a weapon up until I was human
again, then wished for another robe. That green dragon
was my pet all the way to the Plane of Air.

I decided to transfer everything from my Minetown stash
to my lowest one. My dragon killed the Minetown priest,
so I took all the gold I had found in the game down to
the Valley. I was able to make 17 donations, but got
only 1 more point of protection.

I studied up on Master Kaen, and it looked like he
would be just as vulnerable as the other nemeses
I've fought to free action plus wielded potion of
invisibility. That worked liked a charm; I really didn't
get to see what he's capable of. I don't mind that!
Maybe this tactic is overpowered, but why take on
these big bad guys directly?

Gehennom was mostly uneventful, although I
really came to appreciate the Eyes. I ditched my
lamps, and my scrolls and wands of light. It took
a long time to kill one of the demons--I think
Asmodeus--and he gated in Geryon before I was
done. On the Juiblex level I found myself surrounded
by rapidly multiplying gremlins. I used a blessed
scroll of genocide; the other species were
regrettable collateral damage. The only one who
still scares me is Orcus, so I got him with the
paralysis too. In one of the plain Gehennom levels
I had a run-in with a titan; I guess I'm not used to
that since I usually play lawful. He was causing
me a fair bit of trouble, but a black dragon he
had conjured missed me and disintegrated him!
I got the candelabrum and found the vibrating
square (only DL 47). Then I went back up to DL
15 and altar-camped some more. I was nervous
about taking on Rodney. Once you wake him,
there's no more time to spend preparing. My
AC was the worst ever, and I didn't know if I
could handle it.

Thanks to the group's encouragement, I went
for it. I stuck with the amulet of reflection just
in case, but I actually never saw a black dragon
again. I also used the [oMR instead of the robe;
spellcasting is so much less useful once you
have the Amulet anyway--but no one ever
attempted a touch of death! I had plenty of wands
of death, so Rodney never really hurt me. I left my
dragon on DL 47, did the invocation, went down
and back up, then level-teleported just a few
spaces away from the Sanctum temple. That sure
saves time! Another death zap took out the high
priest, then I grabbed the Amulet and used another
cursed !oGL. I don't like to spend a lot of time in the
Sanctum.

Going up was fast and easy; the mysterious
force is much kinder to neutrals than to lawfuls.
I paused on DL 15 to consider what to take. Time
to chuck all the stuff I really wasn't going to use,
including a whole slew of teleportation scrolls.
What I did different this time was stock up on
wands: slow monster, sleep, and especially
teleportation. This turned out to be a great
move, both for Air and Astral. I decided you
just can't count on spellcasting when you have
the Amulet.

Earth was a cinch. Air was something
completely different. I had never tried conflict
there before, and I think that made a big
difference. Of course, I did lose my pet dragon,
but when those elementals keep closing in
there's no time to apply the whistle. Oh what
a difference those wands made! A sleeping
air elemental isn't nearly so scary, and I
could teleport them away from inside. My
HP did drop below 100 once, and I
questioned the wisdom of sticking with
reflection, but I had plenty of potions and I
made it. Fire was very very fast, and Water
is always a piece of cake because I genocide
;.

I took off my ring of conflict before entering
the Astral Plane. I recommend that, so you
can have a little more room to start with.
Checked with telepathy and headed for the
nearest altar, guarded by Famine. As soon
as I was surrounded I put the ring back on.
Used a wand of death on Famine, and then
I just started teleporting away all the bad
guys. What an awesome technique! I had
no idea how much safer Astral could be.
I've never done this before. I recommend it
to absolutely everybody. Just teleport away
all those pesky angels and priests and
assorted enemies. Hurray for wands of
teleportation!

It turned out that first altar was the right
one, so I just finished up. Yay me!

